# `actuarial::Basis`

The mortality and discount assumptions of a valuation, bound together.

## Signature

```cpp
actuarial::Basis actuarial::Basis{actuarial::Mortality mortality, actuarial::Discount discount};
actuarial::Valuation actuarial::operators::value(actuarial::Basis basis);
```

A `Basis` is a plain aggregate of two members, built by naming them in braces: `actuarial::Basis{mortality, discount}`. It performs no calculation: it is the assumption half of a valuation, and [`operators::value`](value.md) turns it into the operator that values cashflows.

## Fields

### `mortality`

`actuarial::Mortality`.

The one-year death probabilities that fix the survival and death probabilities of the valuation, and its maximum horizon. See [`Mortality`](../mortality/Mortality.md). The program reads the horizon back from a basis.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Basis basis{actuarial::mortality::from_qx(pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024})), actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)};
  out.writeln("mortality horizon", basis.mortality.horizon(), " first qx", basis.mortality.qx().items<double>()[0]);
}
```

### `discount`

`actuarial::Discount`.

The interest assumption: a level rate or an explicit curve of factors. See [`Discount`](Discount.md). The program reads the factors back.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Basis basis{actuarial::mortality::from_qx(pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015})), actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)};
  out.writeln("discount factors", basis.discount.factors(3).items<double>());
}
```

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: one basis values every benefit

The same basis values the standard benefits.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  const actuarial::Mortality mortality = actuarial::mortality::from_qx(qx);
  const actuarial::Basis basis{mortality, actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)};
  const actuarial::Valuation value = actuarial::operators::value(basis);
  out.writeln("annuity-due =", value(actuarial::cashflow::annuity(5)).item<double>(), " term =", value(actuarial::cashflow::death(5)).item<double>(), " pure endowment =", value(actuarial::cashflow::survival(5)).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: changing one assumption

Two bases that differ only in the rate, and two that differ only in mortality (one with all deaths doubled).

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  const auto annuity = [&](const pluss::Series &q, double rate) {
    return actuarial::operators::value({actuarial::mortality::from_qx(q), actuarial::discount::flat(rate)})(actuarial::cashflow::annuity(5)).item<double>();
  };
  out.writeln("4%, base mortality   =", annuity(qx, 0.04));
  out.writeln("2%, base mortality   =", annuity(qx, 0.02));
  out.writeln("4%, doubled mortality=", annuity(qx.mul(2.0), 0.04));
}
```

### Example 3: an incomplete basis is refused

A basis whose mortality or discount was never set is refused when the valuation is built.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012});
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::operators::value({actuarial::Mortality{}, actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)}); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::operators::value({actuarial::mortality::from_qx(qx), actuarial::Discount{}}); });
}
```

## Changelog

- **0.1**: initial operator-first actuarial surface.

## See Also

[`Mortality`](../mortality/Mortality.md) and [`Discount`](Discount.md) are its two members, [`operators::value`](value.md) binds it, and [`Cashflow`](Cashflow.md) is what the result values.

